# {{ name }}

Nodeflow nodes for `{{ name }}`, installable into any host that already
requires [`atram/laravel-nodeflow`](https://github.com/atram/laravel-nodeflow)
at `{{ nodeflowConstraint }}`.

There is no manifest here — a host's compatibility comes entirely from
`composer.json`'s own `require` constraint, provider loading comes from
`extra.laravel.providers`, and a node's identity comes from its own `type()`
plus explicit registration below. This package is an ordinary Composer
package that happens to ship Nodeflow nodes, nothing more.

## Installation

```
composer require {{ name }}
```

Laravel's package discovery loads `{{ providerClass }}` automatically from
this package's own `composer.json` — nothing else needs registering.

## Registering nodes

Add each node class to `{{ providerClass }}`'s `$nodes` array:

```php
protected array $nodes = [
    \{{ namespace }}\Nodes\YourNode::class,
];
```

`{{ providerClass }}::boot()` calls `Nodeflow::register($this->nodes)` for
you; nothing further to wire up.

## Why no triggers or subject attributes

This provider carries `$nodes` only — no triggers array, no subject-attribute
method. That is a decision, not an omission: a trigger's `event()` method
names an event class that lives in the *host* application, and a subject
attribute's resolver closure takes the *host*'s own model as its argument.
Neither can be expressed by a class shipped inside a package that has no
knowledge of the host it will eventually be installed into. If your own
workflow needs a trigger or a subject attribute, add it by hand to the host's
own provider — not to this one.

## Running `nodeflow:make-node` here

You cannot run `php artisan nodeflow:make-node` inside this package: the
command hard-codes the host application's own provider path and root
namespace, and this package has no `artisan` of its own. Generate nodes in
your host application and move them here by hand instead. The `$nodes`
array's opening line above is byte-identical to the one the host generator
already knows how to write into, so a future tool that targets a package's
own provider directly would have somewhere to land — but nothing in this
package does that today.
